etl project 


Excellent! ✅ Your environment is ready for our Python + SQL + PostgreSQL ETL project.

We have confirmed that Python is installed and your Ubuntu environment can connect to PostgreSQL on Windows.

Stage 1: Environment Verification — Results
Check	Result	Status
Operating System	Ubuntu on WSL2	Ready
Python	3.12.3	✅ Passed
PostgreSQL host	172.27.80.1	✅ Reachable
PostgreSQL port	5432	✅ Connected
Project directory	~/projects	✅ Available


Step 1: Create the ETL Project

cd ~/projects
mkdir healthcare-etl
cd healthcare-etl
code .

Step 2: Create the Python Virtual Environment

Inside the healthcare-etl directory, execute:

sudo apt update
sudo apt install python3.12-venv
python3 -m venv .venv

Activate the environment:

source .venv/bin/activate


Why do we need a virtual environment?
It keeps this project's Python packages separate from those used by your other Python projects.

Step 3: Install the Required Python Packages

With .venv activated, run:

python -m pip install --upgrade pip

Then install the dependencies:

python -m pip install pandas sqlalchemy "psycopg[binary]" python-dotenv pytest

We will use these packages as follows:

Package	Purpose
Pandas	Transform and clean data
SQLAlchemy	Database connectivity and SQL execution
Psycopg	Connect Python to PostgreSQL
Python-dotenv	Load configuration from environment variables
Pytest	Test our ETL modules



Verify the installations:

python -m pip list

You should see the installed packages in the output.

Step 4: Create the Initial Project Files

Execute the following commands from ~/projects/healthcare-etl:

mkdir -p config
mkdir -p data/input
mkdir -p data/processed
mkdir -p data/rejected
mkdir -p sql
mkdir -p src/extract
mkdir -p src/transform
mkdir -p src/validate
mkdir -p src/load
mkdir -p src/database
mkdir -p src/utils
mkdir -p tests
mkdir -p logs

Create the initial files:

touch config/settings.py
touch main.py
touch requirements.txt
touch .env.example
touch .gitignore
touch README.md

We'll create the Python modules and SQL scripts when we reach their respective stages.

Our initial directory structure will look like this:

healthcare-etl/
├── config/
│   └── settings.py
├── data/
│   ├── input/
│   ├── processed/
│   └── rejected/
├── sql/
├── src/
│   ├── extract/
│   ├── transform/
│   ├── validate/
│   ├── load/
│   ├── database/
│   └── utils/
├── tests/
├── logs/
├── .env.example
├── .gitignore
├── requirements.txt
├── README.md
└── main.py


# Python virtual environment
.venv/

# Environment variables and secrets
.env
.env.*

# Allow the example configuration
!.env.example

# Python cache
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Testing
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# Logs
logs/*.log

# Generated data
data/processed/*
data/rejected/*

# Editor settings
.vscode/


Step 5: Create the PostgreSQL database

Now open pgAdmin 

Execute:
CREATE DATABASE healthcare_etl_db;

INSIDE healthcare_etl_db

Within this database, we'll create four schemas.

Schema -->	Responsibility
source -->	Incoming source data
staging -->	Intermediate ETL data
target -->	Cleaned, validated data
audit -->	ETL execution history and rejected records


CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS source;

CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS staging;

CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS target;

CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS audit;